SECTION 2: ARCHITECTURAL LAYERS (FROZEN ORDER)
2.1 Critical Ordering Principle

RIC (Relational Invariance Constraint) must operate UPSTREAM of optimization and policy.

If RIC comes after weight optimization, it is not a constraint. It is a post-hoc filter.

Constraint boundaries have no power if they follow the decision.
2.2 The Stack (Final)

Layer 0: Base Model

    Generates candidate outputs/actions

    Operates under standard utility optimization logic

    No constraints applied at this level

Layer 1: Relational Invariance Constraint (RIC) — HARD FEASIBILITY GATE

    Evaluates all candidate actions against option-space and agency preservation bounds

    Three components (non-negotiable):

        Agency Preservation

        Epistemic Integrity

        Recoverability

    Actions failing RIC are rejected immediately

    RIC cannot be weight-tuned or overridden by downstream optimization

    RIC is read-only with respect to the weight vector Λ

Layer 2: Logos Kernel (Stability Modules)

    Applies only to actions passing RIC

    Six submodules:

        RCM (Relational Continuity Module)

        NDOM (Non-Destructive Outcome Modeling)

        PRM (Proportional Response Module)

        EDM (Epistemic Dampening Module)

        GBM (Generativity Bias Module)

        APM (Autonomy Preservation Module)

    Reweights utilities among feasible actions

    Does not generate content; does not override policy

Layer 3: Policy & Compliance Layer

    Legal, platform, regulatory enforcement

    Can override RIC only with explicit logged justification

    Override triggers immediate review requirement

    Failed override predictions incur escalating penalties

Layer 4: Renderer

    Tone modulation

    Intensity scaling

    Epistemic calibration

    No constraint modification

2.3 Explicit Guarantee

Once RIC rejects an action, no downstream layer can resurrect it.

The constraint is binary and upstream.
SECTION 3: MATHEMATICAL CORE (FROZEN — NO ADDITIONS)
3.1 Five Core Equations (Only These)

Option-Space: O(s,H) = Set of reachable future states within horizon H Bounded by: physical feasibility, cognitive constraints, resource limits, social constraints

Viability (with Value Definition Layer):

V(s,H) = |O_viable(s,H)| Where O_viable(s,H) = O(s,H) - B B = explicitly declared boundary set of non-viable states B must be: enumerated, version-controlled, publicly auditable, justified

Agency (Two-Component):

A(s) = H(P(actions|s)) × C(s)

Where: H(P) = entropy of available meaningful action distribution C(s) = Changeability Coefficient (probability preference ordering can shift under reflective intervention)

Constraint: High entropy + C ≈ 0 = collapsed agency

Stability Constraint:

Action a is RIC-compliant if:

V_future(s,H) ≥ V_past(s,H) AND A_future(s) ≥ A_past(s)

Subject to Bounded Recoverability Rule (see Section 4)

Harm Tensor (Non-Collapsible):

H⃗ = (H_individual, H_collective, H_minority, H_future)

No single axis may dominate optimization without explicit justification. Imbalance detection monitors axis drift.
3.2 What We Do NOT Add

- No new constraint layers

- No new mathematical formalisms

- No additional stability metrics

- No extended harm dimensions

- No theoretical extensions

These five equations are the complete mathematical core.

